# The three tools the AppImage bundler of tauri-bundler 2.10 (the one
# inside @tauri-apps/cli 2.12.3, see package-lock.json) downloads into
# ~/.cache/tauri when it builds the AppImage, as they were on 2026-10-20,
# except linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage, taken from a tagged release on
# 2026-10-16.
#
# The bundler fetches them from references that can move (release assets
# that can be re-uploaded, a `continuous` release), verifies nothing, runs
# two of them on the build machine and ships AppRun inside the AppImage
# itself. It skips a download when the file already sits in its cache, so
# reproducible/targets/linux.sh downloads the three files itself, checks
# them against this list and only then starts the build. The check must
# come first: once the bundler has linuxdeploy it overwrites three bytes
# at offset 8, to hide the file from AppImage integration tools.
#
# Sources, in the order the bundler names them
# (crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/linuxdeploy.rs, tag
# tauri-cli-v2.12.3 of tauri-apps/tauri):
#   AppRun-x86_64                         https://github.com/tauri-apps/binary-releases/releases/download/apprun-old/AppRun-x86_64
#   linuxdeploy-07333c6-x86_64.AppImage   https://github.com/tauri-apps/binary-releases/releases/download/linuxdeploy-07333c6/linuxdeploy-x86_64.AppImage
#   linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage.AppImage  https://github.com/linuxdeploy/linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage/releases/download/continuous/linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage-x86_64.AppImage
#
# The gtk and gstreamer plugin scripts, two more downloads from `master`
# branches until @tauri-apps/cli 2.11, are now inside the bundler
# (include_bytes!), which writes them to the cache on every build: they
# come with @tauri-apps/cli, whose hash package-lock.json pins. At
# tauri-cli-v2.12.3 their sha256 are
#   ef6b9a980417243bc62e0241b51dc49876032afd1bab9b4762389f961b406d9b  linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh
#   2a15ce9da8de6e20159e1ab27861a7a5ef8758c81a6278ba4ab30cefa1d74c9f  linuxdeploy-plugin-gstreamer.sh
# and the gtk one no longer sets GDK_BACKEND=x11: the AppImage runs on
# Wayland natively when the session is Wayland.
#
# linuxdeploy is now named after the commit it was built from, 07333c6
# (linuxdeploy/linuxdeploy 07333c6e942c7d71782b66be924c5d867f9dfdfc,
# 2026-07-31). The asset was uploaded by FabianLars on 2026-09-20 (asset
# 576945544, GitHub digest sha256:36a2d7e2...) and is byte for byte the
# x86_64 artifact of the upstream CI run 33467564394 (build 368, scheduled,
# 2026-09-01, same commit), compared on 2026-10-20. The release notes name
# run 30674699502 (build 366), whose artifact has other bytes: same
# commit, built a month earlier.
#
# The `continuous` release of linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage is rebuilt on
# the 1st of every month, so its hash changes even when the source does
# not. The plugin is fetched from the release 1-alpha-20250213-1 instead
# (commit a96502d15ce19ec1df3d46e6cbe87ddf38589a71), whose file stays.
# GitHub publishes no digest for it and the upstream run that built it
# has aged out. Its provenance: uploaded by github-actions[bot] on
# 2025-02-13 and never replaced since (asset 228937581), and the same
# hash pinned the next day by KDE Craft (craft-blueprints-kde
# cebd8e789). Its source differs from the `continuous` build pinned
# before (536b068) only in build flags; it bundles appimagetool build
# 227 instead of 295, with the same mksquashfs 4.6.1.
#
# When @tauri-apps/cli moves, read that file again at the matching tag:
# if a URL or a file name changed, update targets/linux.sh and this list.
# To refresh a hash on purpose, download the file, compare it with the
# previous one (a new binary should come with a reason), then run
# `sha256sum` on the three files in the cache directory and paste its
# output below.
f30140a43a0a59e46db21bdefdf749b9e9f2c6946e92afabbacf98b8ae73fb4f  AppRun-x86_64
36a2d7e274d12e1050d0e9ecfe11d339ed54720b2bec464c286d53f8b07f5c62  linuxdeploy-07333c6-x86_64.AppImage
992d502a248e14ab185448ddf6f6e7d25558cb84d4623c354c3af350c25fccb3  linuxdeploy-plugin-appimage.AppImage
